AB  - Human-Oriented Binary Reverse Engineering (HOBRE) aims to transform decompiled pseudocode into a more human-friendly representation, thereby reducing the cognitive burden of reverse analysis and improving efficiency. However, reliably evaluating HOBRE outputs remains a fundamental challenge. On one hand, human evaluation is costly, time-consuming, as well as difficult to scale. On the other hand, existing automated metrics either rely on executable test cases and runtime environments, which are often unavailable for real-world binaries, or depend on high-quality source code references that are typically inaccessible and fail to capture semantically equivalent but lexically diverse outputs. Although the emerging LLM-as-a-Judge paradigm is naturally well-suited to HOBRE evaluation, its effectiveness has not yet been fully studied.
