TI  - DRIFT: hardware-independent peripheral data-flow reconstruction for proactive IoT firmware vulnerability detection in industrial predictive maintenance
AU  - Bin Zeng
T2  - International Conference on Mechanical Engineering, Materials and Automation Technology (MMEAT)
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-30
DO  - 10.1117/12.3124383
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1117/12.3124383
AB  - In advanced manufacturing, MCU-driven IoT devices are widely deployed for intelligent monitoring and predictive maintenance in critical scenarios of smart manufacturing and industrial automation, such as autonomous robotics and process control. Firmware vulnerabilities in these devices have become a core attack surface, directly threatening the security of manufacturing equipment and data integrity. Therefore, developing hardware-independent automated vulnerability detection techniques for IoT device firmware is key to proactively mitigating security risks in this field. Existing mainstream hardware-free solutions struggle to balance accuracy and scalability: firmware-oriented approaches, such as P2IM, rely on heuristic simulation and suffer from insufficient detection accuracy, while hardware abstraction layer-based techniques, such as HALucinator, are constrained by library version and compilation environment consistency, resulting in limited versatility. This paper proposes DRIFT—a data-flow reconstruction-driven detection framework—which, for the first time, integrates static peripheral modeling, model-guided symbolic execution, and constraint-aware directed fuzzing to build a unified, hardware-free platform for predictive maintenance and intelligent monitoring. By automatically deriving peripheral register semantics through cross-function data-flow analysis, DRIFT generates high-fidelity peripheral models without manual configuration, overcoming accuracy bottlenecks and enabling large-scale firmware security audits. Validated on standard industrial firmware test suites consisting of typical milling controllers (CNC) and PLCs in smart manufacturing scenarios, DRIFT achieves 8.3× to 44.5× higher basic block coverage than native QEMU, and 1.1× to 1.6× higher coverage than P2IM, the leading state-of-the-art solution. The framework reproduces all seven vulnerabilities discovered by P2IM and uncovers entirely new composite vulnerabilities involving double free (CWE-415) and out-of-bounds write (CWE-787), thereby providing a scalable, hardware-independent automated vulnerability detection solution for intelligent monitoring and predictive maintenance.
