TI  - Biodigital Diagnosis and Personalized Risk Assessment in Periodontal Diseases
AU  - Ladise Ceylin Has
T2  - Özgür Yayınları eBooks
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-09
DO  - 10.58830/ozgur.pub1444.c5841
UR  - https://doi.org/10.58830/ozgur.pub1444.c5841
AB  - Periodontal diseases are chronic inflammatory conditions shaped by the interplay between dysbiotic microbial communities and host immune-inflammatory responses, together with behavioral, environmental, and systemic factors such as smoking, diabetes, and aging. Their multidimensional nature indicates that periodontal diagnosis should extend beyond the characterization of existing tissue destruction to encompass disease biology, progression risk, and individual patient profiles. The new classification of periodontal and peri-implant diseases, developed at the 2017 World Workshop and published in 2018, introduced a stage- and grade-based framework for periodontitis, enabling the simultaneous assessment of disease severity, treatment complexity, and progression potential. This framework also provides an important foundation for the structured digitization of periodontal records. Electronic periodontal records allow probing depth, clinical attachment loss, bleeding on probing, radiographic bone loss, tooth loss, and individual risk factors to be systematically captured and monitored longitudinally. Digital radiographic analysis and artificial intelligence have substantial potential for the quantitative assessment of alveolar bone loss, periodontal disease classification, and individualized risk prediction. However, their clinical implementation remains constrained by measurement standardization, data quality, external validation, model interpretability, generalizability, patient privacy, and data security. Longitudinal clinical records are particularly valuable for identifying individual patterns of disease progression, while comprehensive risk assessment requires the integration of periodontal history, smoking, diabetes, treatment adherence, and other relevant determinants. The integration of biomarkers, imaging data, clinical measurements, systemic health information, and longitudinal changes may further support the development of individualized periodontal phenotypes. Within this framework, biodigital periodontology represents an emerging and integrative approach with the potential to enhance early and objective diagnosis, personalized risk assessment, clinical decision support, treatment planning, and adaptive supportive periodontal care. Nevertheless, digital technologies and artificial intelligence should be regarded primarily as clinical decision-support tools rather than substitutes for professional judgment. The future of periodontal care will depend not simply on digitizing clinical information, but on transforming heterogeneous, longitudinal, and biologically relevant data into clinically actionable information that enables more precise, individualized, and continuously updated periodontal management.
