AB  - Software vulnerabilities remain a major challenge because security defects can be introduced during ordinary development and may be difficult to identify through manual inspection alone. Traditional static-analysis tools can detect many known weakness patterns, but their warning output can require substantial analyst review. CodeSentinel-AI proposes a comparative framework that evaluates conventional static analysis, classical machine learning, sequence-based deep learning, transformer-based code representations, and graph-based models for automated vulnerability detection and Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) classification. The framework uses source-code tokens, abstract syntax tree information, and contextual representations, with the NIST Juliet Test Suite as the principal benchmark. Evaluation focuses on accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, false-positive rate, false-negative rate, and per-CWE classification performance. An explanation layer identifies code regions associated with model predictions. The numerical tables in this draft are illustrative software-output examples and are not experimental evidence; they must be replaced by results from actual reproducible benchmark runs.
